1
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:03,600
RPO and RTO on the clock. How much data can we lose? How long can service be down?

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:10,400
The operator meant to remove one test event. There is no WHERE clause.

3
00:00:10,900 --> 00:00:13,400
Every event row is deleted, and COMMIT makes the deletion durable.

4
00:00:13,900 --> 00:00:17,800
Nothing crashed. Restarting the server brings nothing back.

5
00:00:18,300 --> 00:00:20,300
A replica copies the deletion too. High availability is not a backup.

6
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:22,800
Recovery needs an earlier state kept OUTSIDE the active database.

7
00:00:25,300 --> 00:00:26,900
An illustrative scenario from the chapter. Follow the arithmetic.

8
00:00:27,400 --> 00:00:29,400
t_r (14:00): the newest state we can actually recover.

9
00:00:29,900 --> 00:00:31,900
t_f (14:17): the failure. The harmful DELETE commits.

10
00:00:32,400 --> 00:00:34,400
14:21: an operator notices. Users were affected before this.

11
00:00:34,900 --> 00:00:37,300
t_a (14:29): restore and checks done. Service is acceptable again.

12
00:00:37,800 --> 00:00:41,000
Data-loss window: 14:17 − 14:00 = 17 minutes of changes this export can't return.

13
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:45,500
Recovery duration: 14:29 − 14:17 = 12 minutes, measured from the failure.

14
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:51,600
Starting at 14:21 gives 8 minutes and hides 4 minutes of detection delay.

15
00:00:52,100 --> 00:00:54,100
Suppose the service promised an RPO of 5 minutes.

16
00:00:54,600 --> 00:00:58,200
17 minutes of possible loss is more than 5. The RPO is missed.

17
00:00:58,700 --> 00:01:00,300
And an RTO of 10 minutes: 12 is more than 10. Missed again.

18
00:01:00,800 --> 00:01:03,200
We did recover. We did not keep the promise. Those are different outcomes.

19
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:11,900
The same 17-minute gap can hold 3 writes or thousands. Time is not a row count.

20
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:14,800
A logical export represents its consistent snapshot, not the time the file finished.

21
00:01:15,300 --> 00:01:17,700
Other history sources may hold some later writes. State which sources your numbers assume.

22
00:01:19,300 --> 00:01:29,300
Remember: A committed mistake survives restarts and is copied by replicas. RPO limits the data-loss window:  t_f − t_r  (failure − newest recoverable state). RTO limits the recovery duration:  t_a − t_f  (start the clock at the failure). Writing a target down does not meet it. Measure it in a restore drill. A time gap is not a row count. Say which state your artifact represents.

